 ### Hybrid Splatting/Tracing Rendering
-A rendering pipeline that rasterizes primary rays for speed and traces secondary rays for lighting effects (reflections/refractions) within the same 3D representation [S2].
-*   **Primary Rays:** Compute all primary ray intersections with the scene, render these using rasterization, and discard Gaussian hits that fall behind a ray's closest intersection [S2].
-*   **Secondary Rays:** Compute and trace secondary rays using 3DGRT to enable complex visual effects like reflections and refractions [S2].
+- [S2] A rendering pipeline that rasterizes primary rays for speed and traces secondary rays for lighting effects (reflections/refractions) within the same 3D representation.
+- [S2] Compute all primary ray intersections with the scene.
+- [S2] Render these primary rays using rasterization (splatting).
+- [S2] Discard Gaussian hits that fall behind a ray's closest intersection.
+- [S2] Compute and trace secondary rays using 3DGRT.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the fundamental volume rendering equation [G]. Hardware-accelerated raycasting became feasible with the advent of programmable fragment shaders (GLSL/HLSL) in the mid-2000s, allowing per-pixel ray marching on the GPU [S2]. Recent developments integrate these techniques with neural fields and Gaussian splatting, enabling hybrid rendering pipelines that combine rasterization speed with ray-tracing accuracy [S2].
+The concept of ray casting for volume rendering has been a standard technique for high-quality visualization, leveraging the parallel nature of GPU fragment shaders to perform per-pixel ray marching [S2]. The specific GPU implementation pipeline described in [S2] utilizes a slab method for AABB intersection and front-to-back compositing within a single fragment shader pass. The integration of ray tracing APIs like NVIDIA OptiX [S1, unofficial] allows for more complex traversal and shading models, while recent hybrid approaches [S2] combine the speed of rasterization for primary rays with the accuracy of ray tracing for secondary effects like reflections.
 
